<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs findings that are decisions rather than defects.

  Every entry names the decision. An exclusion without a reason is indistinguishable from a bug
  someone got tired of, and a filter nobody can audit is worse than no filter: it makes the next
  real finding easy to add to the list.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

  <!--
    FindSecBugs flags every log call with a non-constant argument. Almost all of them are ids,
    enums and counters. The two paths that log model- or tool-controlled text — the interpreter's
    TRACE payloads and the OpenAI-compatible adapter's wire bodies — fold every line terminator
    to a literal \n first (see boundForTrace in each), so a tool result cannot forge a log line.
    That is the property this rule is about, and it is enforced at those call sites.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="CRLF_INJECTION_LOGS"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The vocabulary here is records and immutable value types: a compact constructor runs
    List.copyOf / Map.copyOf / Set.copyOf on every collection it takes, and no component is an
    array. What SpotBugs sees is the accessor returning the same reference it was given, which
    for an already-immutable copy is the intended behaviour. Verified: no finding in either
    family involves an array.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    "Constructor throws" is a finalizer-attack concern, and these types have no finalizer: they
    are records and final classes that validate their arguments in the compact constructor. That
    validation is the reason a malformed ThreadId or CapabilityId cannot exist at all.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    IMPROPER_UNICODE fires on case-insensitive comparison of attacker-influenced text. Every
    remaining site compares against an ASCII-only vocabulary the harness itself defines — event
    types, header names, enum names — and the ones where a locale could change the answer now
    pass Locale.ROOT explicitly.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="IMPROPER_UNICODE"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    No statement is built from anything a run supplies. A table name comes from
    SqlSchema.tableFor, a static map keyed by a store name that JdbcRowStore has already matched
    against [a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}; the DDL is a constant. Every value is a bind parameter.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SQL_INJECTION_SPRING_JDBC"/>
    <Or>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.storage.sql.JdbcRowStore"/>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.storage.sql.SqlSchema"/>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.storage.projection.JdbcRunProjectionCache"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Running a command is what these two are for. ShellTool is the `builtin.shell` lane, gated by
    the capability host and approved per exact invocation; StdioTransport spawns the MCP server
    the operator configured. Neither goes through a shell — both take argv as a list, which is
    the mitigation this rule asks for.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="COMMAND_INJECTION"/>
    <Or>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.tools.ShellTool"/>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.tools.mcp.StdioTransport"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>

  <!--
    A SecureRandom used once is not the weakness this rule describes: the finding is about
    java.util.Random being seeded and discarded. VaultKey mints one 32-byte key, once, when the
    key file does not yet exist.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="DMI_RANDOM_USED_ONLY_ONCE"/>
    <Or>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.storage.secret.VaultKey"/>
      <Class name="io.jclaw.app.cli.RoutinesCommand$Add"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>

  <!--
    "[a-z][a-z0-9_]*(\.[a-z][a-z0-9_]*)+" cannot backtrack catastrophically: every repetition of
    the group must start with a literal dot, so no input can be split between the group and its
    successor in more than one way. Pinned by CapabilityIdTest.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="REDOS"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.contracts.capability.CapabilityId"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The "password" is the H2 URL's empty credential for an embedded file database. There is no
    secret here to hard-code; a real deployment supplies a JDBC URL and the driver's own
    properties.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="HARD_CODE_PASSWORD"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.app.config.JclawConfiguration"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    `jclaw onboard` writes a YAML file. Its newlines must be \n, which is what the text block
    produces; %n would emit the platform separator into a file format that does not want one.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="VA_FORMAT_STRING_USES_NEWLINE"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.app.cli.OnboardCommand"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Paths whose parent is missing. Each of these resolves under a directory the harness owns —
    the state directory, the workspace root, an extracted package — so the parent exists by
    construction, and a null there would mean the configuration is already broken.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Returning null from a host function is Endive's contract for a WebAssembly import that
    declares no results. A zero-length array would be a different thing to the runtime.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="PZLA_PREFER_ZERO_LENGTH_ARRAYS"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.tools.wasm.WasmLane"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The browser UI is one HTML string. It is duplicated across class files because javac interns
    it into every class that references the constant; it is 9 KB, loaded once, in a process that
    holds an agent's transcript.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="HSC_HUGE_SHARED_STRING_CONSTANT"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.app.http.WebUi"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Two switch arms with the same body: the inbound policy's WARN and its unreachable OFF now
    differ (OFF throws), and this one is the interpreter's handling of two decisions that
    genuinely do the same thing. Kept as separate arms because collapsing them would hide which
    decisions exist.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="DB_DUPLICATE_SWITCH_CLAUSES"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.domain.safety.InboundScreening"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The only variable in these format strings is a column width, computed from the longest
    capability id in the table. Nothing a run supplies reaches them.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="FORMAT_STRING_MANIPULATION"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.app.cli.ToolsCommand"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    A synthetic local from the desugaring of a pattern switch over a sealed hierarchy, not a
    store anyone wrote. The method's own source has no dead assignment in it.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="DLS_DEAD_LOCAL_STORE"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.providers.anthropic.AnthropicModelProvider"/>
    <Method name="toMessageParam"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    A discarded return value that is doing work: building the spec is what validates a WASM
    package's declared permissions, and the spec a call runs under is built later from the
    operator's configuration. Removing the call once silently removed the check — the test
    WasmExtensionIntegrationTest.refusals is what noticed.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED_INFERRED"/>
    <Class name="io.jclaw.storage.extension.FilesystemExtensionRegistry"/>
  </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
